The Sleep(1) Issue

Sleep(1) is notorious in Windows programming because the actual sleep duration depends on the OS scheduler resolution (which defaults to 15.6ms, though games often set it to 1ms using timeBeginPeriod). If the background thread receives the packet

        
0.1 ms0.1 ms

      

after the loop starts, the game thread might still sleep for a full millisecond or more before checking again.

    To optimize the threaded model: Consider replacing Sleep(1) in the spin-wait loop with a lightweight yield like SwitchToThread() or YieldProcessor() (which performs a PAUSE instruction on modern CPUs). This allows the game thread to react almost instantly when the background thread releases the lock, saving sub-millisecond delays.




